counterfactuals.output:
A RAID decoding apparatus comprising:

a storage module configured to store a plurality of data strips and parity data associated with a RAID stripe, the parity data corresponding to first and second Reed-Solomon codewords;

a Reed-Solomon (RS) decoder coupled to the storage module and configured to decode the first and second Reed-Solomon codewords; and

a multiplexer coupled between the storage module and the RS decoder and configured to selectively provide, to the RS decoder, syndrome information derived from the parity data;

wherein the apparatus is configured to generate updated syndromes for each of the first and second Reed-Solomon codewords based at least in part on the parity data stored in the storage module;

wherein the updated syndromes comprise a first updated syndrome corresponding to the first Reed-Solomon codeword and a second updated syndrome corresponding to the second Reed-Solomon codeword;

wherein the multiplexer is configured to selectively output the first updated syndrome and the second updated syndrome to the RS decoder; and

wherein the RS decoder is configured to perform error correction on the first and second Reed-Solomon codewords using the respective updated syndromes.

In one embodiment, the storage module stores original parity symbols and updated parity symbols generated after a write operation to one or more data strips of the RAID stripe. The updated syndromes are generated by modifying previously computed syndromes using syndrome contributions obtained from the updated parity data, thereby avoiding full recomputation of syndromes from all symbols of the corresponding Reed-Solomon codewords.

In another embodiment, the multiplexer is controlled to provide the RS decoder with syndrome values associated with a selected one of the two Reed-Solomon codewords during a decoding interval. The RS decoder may thereby be shared between the first and second Reed-Solomon codewords while maintaining separate syndrome information for each codeword.

In operation, parity data read from the storage module is used to update syndrome values for both Reed-Solomon codewords. The updated syndrome values are then applied to the RS decoder through the multiplexer. The RS decoder determines error locations and error magnitudes based on the updated syndromes and corrects erroneous symbols in the corresponding Reed-Solomon codewords.
